From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Wed, 19 Aug 2026 13:34:35 +0200
Subject: dt-bindings: leds: lgm: describe the binding the driver implements

There is no device tree that satisfies both this schema and the driver
it documents, and nothing has noticed because intel,lgm-ssoled has no
in-tree DTS user anywhere. Four separate defects, each independently
fatal:

  - compatible is a const, which forbids the "syscon" string. The
    driver's only route to its registers is
    syscon_node_to_regmap(dev->of_node), and that returns -EINVAL
    unless of_device_is_compatible(np, "syscon") holds. A node written
    to the schema does not probe.

  - the LED container is documented as led-controller, while the driver
    asks for a child node named exactly ssoled. Nothing warns about the
    difference: sso_led_dt_parse() returns 0 when the named child is
    absent, so a DT written to the schema registers no LEDs and reports
    success.

  - the per-LED gpio property is documented as led-gpios, but the
    driver calls devm_fwnode_gpiod_get() with a NULL con_id, which
    looks for "gpios" and then "gpio". The documented name arrived in
    commit 121164481b79 ("dt-bindings: leds: intel,lgm: Add missing
    'led-gpios' property"), a reasonable schema fix that nothing then
    checked against the code.

  - reg is capped at 2 and the node name pattern at ^led@[0-2]$. That
    is one board's LED count masquerading as a property of the block:
    reg is the controller pin number, the block has 32 pins and the
    driver bounds it against SSO_LED_MAX_NUM. Both the cap and the
    pattern have to move, since children above led@2 fail the pattern
    independently of the cap.

Fix all four against the driver rather than against any board, and add
a second compatible while here: the same block appears on Intel's
GRX500 family, where the shift clock runs on the other edge. The driver
patch that follows keys the edge off the compatible; nothing else about
the two differs, which is why they share this binding rather than
getting a second one.

The example moves with the schema, and its own long-standing
inconsistency goes with it -- led@2 declared reg = <2> while pointing
its gpio at pin 23, so the node named one pin and drove another.

Fixes: b113a7f19810 ("dt-bindings: leds: Add bindings for Intel LGM SoC")
Fixes: 121164481b79 ("dt-bindings: leds: intel,lgm: Add missing 'led-gpios' property")
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-fable-5
Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>

diff --git a/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/leds/leds-lgm.yaml b/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/leds/leds-lgm.yaml
index 8b3b3bf1eaf2..a5416d4ba19a 100644
--- a/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/leds/leds-lgm.yaml
+++ b/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/leds/leds-lgm.yaml
@@ -4,15 +4,28 @@
 $id: http://devicetree.org/schemas/leds/leds-lgm.yaml#
 $schema: http://devicetree.org/meta-schemas/core.yaml#
 
-title: Intel Lightning Mountain (LGM) SoC LED Serial Shift Output (SSO) Controller driver
+title: Intel LGM and GRX500 SoC LED Serial Shift Output (SSO) Controller
 
 maintainers:
   - Zhu, Yi Xin <Yixin.zhu@intel.com>
   - Amireddy Mallikarjuna reddy <mallikarjunax.reddy@intel.com>
 
+description: |
+  The controller shifts a 32 bit output word out to an external shift
+  register, one bit per pin, and holds an eight bit duty cycle per pin.
+
+  The node is also a syscon: the driver reaches the register block through
+  syscon_node_to_regmap(), which refuses a node whose compatible does not
+  carry the "syscon" string, so that string is part of the binding rather
+  than an option.
+
 properties:
   compatible:
-    const: intel,lgm-ssoled
+    items:
+      - enum:
+          - intel,grx500-ssoled
+          - intel,lgm-ssoled
+      - const: syscon
 
   reg:
     maxItems: 1
@@ -40,7 +53,7 @@ properties:
     description:
       Blink frequency for SOUTs in Hz.
 
-  led-controller:
+  ssoled:
     type: object
     description:
       This sub-node must contain a sub-node for each leds.
@@ -55,17 +68,17 @@ properties:
         const: 0
 
     patternProperties:
-      "^led@[0-2]$":
+      "^led@1?[0-9a-f]$":
         $ref: common.yaml#
         unevaluatedProperties: false
 
         properties:
           reg:
-            description: Index of the LED.
+            description: Number of the controller pin driving this LED.
             minimum: 0
-            maximum: 2
+            maximum: 31
 
-          led-gpios:
+          gpios:
             maxItems: 1
 
           intel,sso-hw-trigger:
@@ -103,7 +116,7 @@ examples:
     #include <dt-bindings/leds/common.h>
 
     ssogpio: ssogpio@e0d40000 {
-      compatible = "intel,lgm-ssoled";
+      compatible = "intel,lgm-ssoled", "syscon";
       reg = <0xE0D40000 0x2E4>;
       gpio-controller;
       #gpio-cells = <2>;
@@ -114,7 +127,7 @@ examples:
       clock-names = "sso", "fpid";
       intel,sso-update-rate-hz = <250000>;
 
-      led-controller {
+      ssoled {
         #address-cells = <1>;
         #size-cells = <0>;
 
@@ -122,14 +135,14 @@ examples:
           reg = <0>;
           function = "gphy";
           color = <LED_COLOR_ID_GREEN>;
-          led-gpios = <&ssogpio 0 0>;
+          gpios = <&ssogpio 0 0>;
         };
 
         led@2 {
           reg = <2>;
           function = LED_FUNCTION_POWER;
           color = <LED_COLOR_ID_GREEN>;
-          led-gpios = <&ssogpio 23 0>;
+          gpios = <&ssogpio 2 0>;
         };
       };
     };
